Knowledge is important , but empathy and love are crucial to encouraging a skeptic to return to the faith . 1 2
This is often a necessary step , and it stands as a reminder that faith is a gift and not simply something that can be reasoned to by one’s own intellect .
Indeed , some skeptics are angry or disappointed at their own or others’ misfortunes , but many other skeptics simply don’t believe that God exists .
There is an important truth every believer needs to know to help recover a lost soul .
He takes away every branch in me that does not bear fruit , and every one that does he prunes so that it bears more fruit .
I am the vine , you are the branches .
” So , what can you do to help the nonbeliever ?
It includes maintaining an active , daily prayer life and living your faith .
More specifically , pray to God for the nonbeliever and love him or her .
“They will know you by your love .
While these actions may seem less satisfying than engaging in persuasive debate and forceful argument , they are crucial .
there was someone praying for that person and giving example through their life and actions that ultimately caused the skeptic to reevaluate his beliefs .
So , before considering other conversion methods , never underestimate the power of prayer and the power of love .
Monica what she could do to bring her son , St .
Seeing the mother’s love for her son , Ambrose replied , “it is impossible that a son of so many tears should be lost.”
After struggling with doubts for decades and answering many of his own questions and challenges , Augustine did convert .
The then-Bishop Ambrose’s approach is probably the best advice anyone has ever given on the subject .
There is an ongoing dispute about whether one can be save by faith alone or if faith must be accompanied by works .
The Church teaches :
Grace is favor , the free and undeserved help that God gives us to respond to his call to become children of God , adoptive sons , partakers of the divine nature and of eternal life ”
Christians believe in the necessity of God’s grace for salvation , but there are different ideas about what that means .
It doesn’t merely cover over our sinfulness , but truly transforms us and makes us holy .
So , we can play an active role in our salvation—but a role totally dependent upon God’s grace ; we can’t save ourselves .
The guilt and effects of the sin of Adam and Eve were passed down to the entire human race ( see Genesis 3:16-19).
Man who was at one time the favored creature of God , found himself doomed to suffer in disgrace , utterly incapable of restoring the friendship with His Maker that had been severed by disobedience .
Redemption ( via Crucifixion and Resurrection )
As Saint John wrote , “ For God so loved the world that he gave his only Son that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life .
In turn , the Son of God , who was both fully-God and fully-man , would freely offer Himself as a Sacrifice to God , rectifying man’s defiance with an act of perfect obedience as Paul mentions in his Letters to the Romans 5:15, Colossians (1:19-20), and Hebrews 2:9.
Had he merely become the semblance of a man , as some have maintained , His Sacrifice on our behalf would have been real , i.e. , he would not have been losing anything , but as a man , he lost his life .
So , Jesus ’ crucifixion and death form the paradox of all paradoxes .
Because crucifixion was reserved for the most heinous criminals , the thought of worshiping someone who had died in this manner would seem ludicrous to many of his contemporaries .
Christ’s meritorious death is symbolized as well in the bronze serpent mounted on a pole , which the Lord instructed Moses to fashion so that those bitten by snakes might look upon it and live ( see the Book of Numbers , 21:8-9, and John 3:14-15). Resurrection
As Saint Paul wrote in his First Letter to the Corinthians 15:14, “If Christ has not been raised then our preaching is in vain and your faith is in vain.” Eyewitness Testimony
That the initial testimony to the Resurrection , the Faith’s foundational truth , was entrusted to women is highly significant .
The Grace of God
It makes sense that fallen men – us – are unable to approach Him in that condition .
As Saint Peter wrote said , “Repent , and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness of your sins and you shall receive the gift of the Holy Spirit.” ( See the Acts of the Apostles , 2:38, and Mark 16:16).
Scripture further teaches that the good we do on earth shall be rewarded in Heaven .
Again , it is important to remember that the merit we receive comes not from the acts in and of themselves , but from the act of Christ’s saving death on Calvary .
For example , Saint Justin the Martyr explained in about 150 A.D. , for instance , “Every man will receive the eternal punishment or reward which his actions deserve” ( First Apology 12).
Some try to show that faith alone is sufficient for salvation by citing Saint Paul’s letter to the Ephesians , 2:8-9: “For by grace you have been saved through faith ; and this is not your own doing , it is the gift of God—not because of works , lest any man should boast.” However , that sentence should be read in context .
This sort of legalistic thinking sets up a strict servant-master relationship with God , as though one may approach Him on Judgment Day and demand payment for services rendered , reducing salvation to a kind of spiritual business transaction !
That Paul believed good works are essential to salvation is evident in the verse immediately following Ephesians 2:9, which states , “We are his workmanship , created in Christ Jesus for good works , which God prepared beforehand , that we should walk in them.”
To neglect to do good , therefore , is to fail to love God .
So , the two “ greatest ” commandments – love God and love your neighbor – mutually support each other .
Ironically , though , as we quoted above , the one place where the phrase “ faith alone ” appears in Scripture is in the Letter of James , which states , “You see that a man is justified by works and not by faith alone” (2:24, emphasis added ), which , of course , is the exact opposite that some would have you believe .
It has to be done for the right reasons .
Their teachings are not mutually exclusive ; they are complementary .
We would argue , meaningless and void .
The Lord earned infinitely more merit than would be required to save every human being who has ever lived or ever will live ; and nothing more is needed .
God wants us to participate in His work , not out of necessity but out of love and the desire to bestow dignity upon us so that we are more than animals .
In that way , it is not by our own merits that we are called to , carry out , and complete good works , but it is through the recognition that it is through those efforts and what Christ won for us on the Cross .
The early Christians regularly made the pious gesture known as the Sign of the Cross , which endures today as one of the most distinguishing marks of the Christian Faith .
You know that I love you.” He said to him, “Feed my sheep.
And when he had said this, he said to him, “Follow me.”
Purgatory is not an alternative to heaven or hell .
As the Second Vatican Council taught , purgatory exists because “ even when the guilt of sin has been taken away , punishment for it or the consequences of it may remain to be expiated or cleansed ” ( Indulgentiarum Doctrina 3).
Moreover , the Church accepts the biblical truth that spiritual perfection is required for admittance into heaven , for per our above reference to the Book the Revelation (21:27), “nothing unclean shall enter it.”
Similarly , one of the more stinging stories in scripture well illustrates this notion forgiveness and consequences .
Forgiveness and Consequences The story of Bathsheba and David and Nathan tells us a great deal about the nature of sin and the mercy of God .
God was eager and willing to forgive and restore , but there had to be consequences .
We may wonder , what exactly are the effects and consequences if , in fact , all sin was atoned on the cross ?
Think of any number of sins ( and crimes ) like murder , arson and assault .
So , forgiveness then , does not necessarily mean that the consequences are removed .
To understand how punishment could remain even after one’s sins have been forgiven , it is necessary to distinguish between eternal and temporal punishment .
One is saved from this punishment by God when he – the sinner – repents and confesses those sins .
Consider , for example , the husband who is unfaithful to his wife .
His wife , in her goodness , forgives him , however , it may be a long time before she will trust him again .
When we sin we hurt our relationship with God and others .
Of course , this healing occurs by the grace of God through the merits of Jesus Christ’s death on the Cross .
To be clear , Purgatory has nothing to do with the forgiveness of sin because the sins of the souls in purgatory have already been forgiven .
Again , these souls are saved , but their entry into heaven is delayed .
But we know this : that no penalty presses so hard upon the “poor souls” as the consciousness that they are by their own fault long debarred from the blessed Vision of God .
It is homesickness for their Father ; and the further their purification proceeds , the more painfully are their souls scourged with its rods of fire…
There are many Scripture passages that allude to a form of expiation of sin after death .
In the Old Testament there is the account of Judas Maccabeus who “ made atonement for the dead , that they might be delivered from their sin ” ( see the Second Book of Maccabees 12:46).
Yet even if one does not believe these books to be inspired by God , he should at least consider the historical witness they provide .
This is substantiated by Second Book of Samuel 1:12, which tells us David and his men “ mourned and wept and fasted until evening for ( the soldiers of the Lord ) because they had fallen by the sword. ”
Paul utters a prayer for the dead in his Second Letter to Timothy , saying of his deceased friend Onesiphorus , “ May the Lord grant him to find mercy from the Lord that Day ” (1:18).
In the first case , the work the Christian has carried out in life survives judgment and he goes directly to his heavenly reward , i.e. , verse 14.
In Matthew 12:32 Jesus seems to imply there is reparation for sin beyond death : “ Whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven , either in this age or in the age to come ” ( emphasis added ).
Elsewhere , Jesus implies that some of the deceased shall undergo varying degrees of temporal punishment ( see Luke 12:47-48). Early Christian References to Purgatory
Shortly before her death , it was revealed to her that he had entered into paradise .
Most profoundly , we see the early Christian practice of offering the Eucharistic Sacrifice on behalf of the dead .
So Where Does that Leave Us ?
