We are less concerned with who gave the sermon and more interested in the message and its consistency with the central tenets or practical application of our Roman Catholic faith .
And , why is Mary’s virginity so important to Catholics ?
Scripturally , one might reflect upon the story of Moses and the burning bush .
This story helps us to understand Mary’s Perpetual Virginity in two ways .
We should not forget that this same God , who appeared to Moses in the burning bush , was conceived in the womb of Mary .
Second , the Church Fathers saw the image of the burning bush itself – a bush aflame , yet not consumed – as a metaphor of Mary’s giving birth without forfeiting her virginity .
… As on the mountain the bush burned but was not consumed , so the Virgin gave birth to the light and was not corrupted ” ( On the Birth of Christ ).
Again , consider Ezekiel , “ shall go out , and after he has gone out the gate shall be shut.
Isaiah , after all , affirms her virginity in conceiving and in bearing .
Mary’s response , to the Archangel’s announcement she would conceive and bear a Son –“ How can this be since I do not know man ?” ( Luke 1:34)– clearly suggests that she was a virgin .
Her perpetually virginal state is implied in the Song of Solomon , which says , “ A garden locked is my sister , my bride , a fountain sealed ” (4:12).
