It is painful to sever our undue attachments to the things of this world , but the reward that awaits us in the world to come is worth the cost .
It is for discipline that you have to endure .
His Sacrifice on our behalf is in itself perfectly complete and efficacious .
For example , some see pain and want as punishments meted out by God upon the faithless , or suffering and eventual death from , say , lung cancer as brought on by a personal lack of faith .
Near the end of his life , the same Peter , who had once been rebuked by Jesus for wanting Him to avoid suffering , declared to the faithful :
For the worker is worthy of his pay .
It is almost funny , but Jesus’ discourse on marriage caused some of His followers to conclude it was better for one not to marry .
“Not all men can receive this saying , but only those to whom it is given .
The Apostle Paul recommended celibacy as well to whomever could accept it .
The unmarried man is anxious about the affairs of the Lord , how to please the Lord ; but the married man is anxious about worldly affairs , how to please his wife , and his interests are divided.” (7:32-34)
Also in the Apostle’s letters we find reference to the Order of Widows .
“Honor widows who are real widows … She who is a real widow , and is left all alone , has set her hope on God and continues in supplications and prayers night and day ; whereas she who is self-indulgent is even while she lives .
It was the duty of consecrated widows , then , to pray continuously as well as to live ascetic lives with set regulations .
“ Let a widow be enrolled , if she is not less than sixty years of age , having been the wife of one husband ; 10 and she must be well attested for her good deeds , as one who has brought up children , shown hospitality , washed the feet of the saints , relieved the afflicted , and devoted herself to doing good in every way .
These celibate women , dedicated to lives of prayer and charity , formed the first consecrated female religious order ; these were the first nuns .
Nevertheless , though not yet mandatory , the Apostolic Church clearly preferred celibacy for the clergy ( see Matthew 19:12 and The First Letter to the Corinthians 7:32, 38).
Despite the fact that married men were admitted to the clergy in the first centuries , moreover , unmarried men were prohibited from marrying once they had been ordained and widowed priests were to remarry .2 The Church’s constant reverence for religious celibacy is well-documented from her earliest days forward .
Anti-Catholics have misused Paul’s reproach of those “who forbid marriage” ( First Letter to Timothy 4:3) to attack the Church’s stance on celibacy .
To the contrary , the Catholic Church , far from being opposed to marriage , esteems Holy Matrimony as a Sacrament and a sanctified state .
Celibacy “for the sake of the kingdom of heaven” ( Matthew 19:12) is favorable to God for it points to the manner in which the Saints in Heaven live ( see Matthew 22:30).
“It is these , ” writes Saint John , “who have not defiled themselves with women , for they are chaste ; it is these who follow the Lamb wherever he goes ; these have been redeemed from mankind as first fruits for God and the Lamb , and in their mouth no lie was found , for they are spotless” (14:4-5).
It is interesting to note , however , that his wife is not mentioned in the story , leading some scholars to conclude she had perished before Peter entered the ministry .
It remains the practice in the Eastern Rites for those who enter into the priesthood in the unmarried state to remain unmarried .
A small number of former Lutheran and Anglican ministers , who were married prior to reconciliation with the Catholic Faith , have been granted a special dispensation to serve as priests .
